  "summary": "Traditional earth-building technique using mud bricks and plaster prevalent in Sahelian regions.",
  "description": "Characterized by thick earthen walls with smooth, sculpted surfaces and organic forms, developed due to scarcity of stone and wood in the region. This technique represents an adaptation to local climate and resources, creating naturally insulating structures.",
  "culturalContext": "Banco masonry — sun-dried mud brick set in earth mortar and finished in smooth plaster — is the load-bearing technology of the West African Sahel, maintained by mason lineages for centuries and refreshed seasonally by whole communities. From Djenné houses to mosque walls across Mali, Burkina Faso and northern Nigeria, banco answers the Sahel's extremes: thermal mass against heat, materials from the field itself, and repair built into yearly ritual.",
